WEBVTT

00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:02.448
Singly linked lists are used

00:00:02.525 --> 00:00:04.519
to build other structures.

00:00:04.519 --> 00:00:07.358
A stack pushes and pops at the head

00:00:07.358 --> 00:00:10.197
both O(1): that is lecture nine.

00:00:10.273 --> 00:00:12.345
A queue inserts at the tail

00:00:12.421 --> 00:00:15.260
and deletes at the head: lecture ten.

00:00:15.610 --> 00:00:18.658
Hash tables keep all the keys that hash

00:00:18.736 --> 00:00:21.002
to the same slot in one list

00:00:21.002 --> 00:00:23.034
called separate chaining

00:00:23.034 --> 00:00:24.675
in lecture eighteen.

00:00:24.675 --> 00:00:27.879
The slide says this prevents collisions

00:00:27.879 --> 00:00:30.848
more exactly, chaining resolves them

00:00:30.848 --> 00:00:34.130
the colliding keys share one slot's list.

00:00:34.480 --> 00:00:35.975
And undo in an editor

00:00:36.043 --> 00:00:38.285
each new action goes on the front

00:00:38.353 --> 00:00:40.120
of a list of past actions.

00:00:40.700 --> 00:00:43.174
Here is the complete class from the

00:00:43.245 --> 00:00:45.225
slides' Java implementation.

00:00:45.225 --> 00:00:46.921
Its only field is head

00:00:46.921 --> 00:00:49.467
a reference to the first real node

00:00:49.467 --> 00:00:50.880
no dummy this time.

00:00:50.880 --> 00:00:53.779
So the empty list is head equal to null.

00:00:53.850 --> 00:00:55.900
insertFirst and deleteFirst

00:00:55.971 --> 00:00:57.243
work at the front

00:00:57.243 --> 00:01:00.071
insertLast and deleteLast at the end

00:01:00.142 --> 00:01:02.828
and insertAfter and deleteAfter next

00:01:02.899 --> 00:01:05.020
to a node holding a given key.

00:01:05.370 --> 00:01:07.343
search and printLinkedList

00:01:07.413 --> 00:01:09.175
visit the nodes in order.

00:01:09.175 --> 00:01:12.346
You saw how each of these works in part two

00:01:12.346 --> 00:01:14.530
now look at the special cases.

00:01:15.110 --> 00:01:17.365
The slide version has two bugs

00:01:17.365 --> 00:01:19.112
both on the empty list.

00:01:19.112 --> 00:01:21.585
deleteFirst reads head.next

00:01:21.585 --> 00:01:24.641
but when the list is empty, head is null

00:01:24.714 --> 00:01:27.188
and reading a field of null throws

00:01:27.260 --> 00:01:29.079
a NullPointerException.

00:01:29.079 --> 00:01:32.353
The fix is to check for an empty list first

00:01:32.353 --> 00:01:33.590
and return null.

00:01:33.940 --> 00:01:36.490
insertLast has the same problem

00:01:36.567 --> 00:01:38.499
it reads current.next

00:01:38.576 --> 00:01:40.276
while current is null.

00:01:40.276 --> 00:01:43.290
The fix: on an empty list the new node

00:01:43.367 --> 00:01:45.222
simply becomes the head.

00:01:45.222 --> 00:01:48.313
Always test a method on the empty list

00:01:48.313 --> 00:01:50.940
and on a list of one node, first.

00:01:51.520 --> 00:01:53.972
The slides' test program.

00:01:53.972 --> 00:01:56.816
insertFirst(5), then insertFirst(6)

00:01:56.816 --> 00:02:00.641
which goes in front of it, then insertFirst(7)

00:02:00.641 --> 00:02:01.622
then insertFirst(1).

00:02:01.622 --> 00:02:04.760
Each new node becomes the head.

00:02:05.110 --> 00:02:08.822
insertLast(2) walks to 5

00:02:08.950 --> 00:02:11.894
and links 2 after it.

00:02:11.894 --> 00:02:17.654
So the list prints as head, 1, 7, 6

00:02:17.654 --> 00:02:19.830
5, 2, null.

00:02:20.410 --> 00:02:23.350
deleteAfter(1) finds the

00:02:23.463 --> 00:02:26.063
first node holding 1

00:02:26.063 --> 00:02:31.038
and makes it skip the node after it, 7.

00:02:31.038 --> 00:02:34.090
Head, 1, 6, 5, 2.

00:02:34.440 --> 00:02:37.482
insertAfter(5, 9)

00:02:37.482 --> 00:02:41.538
links a new node in after 5.

00:02:41.538 --> 00:02:45.720
Head, 1, 6, 5, 9, 2.

00:02:46.070 --> 00:02:48.347
deleteFirst() returns 1

00:02:48.347 --> 00:02:50.715
and 6 becomes the head.

00:02:50.806 --> 00:02:52.992
deleteLast() walks to the

00:02:53.084 --> 00:02:56.727
second-to-last node, 9, cuts off 2

00:02:56.727 --> 00:02:58.184
and returns it.

00:02:58.184 --> 00:03:01.190
The list is now 6, 5, 9.

00:03:01.540 --> 00:03:05.275
search(9) is true; search(7) is false

00:03:05.275 --> 00:03:07.620
because 7 was deleted.

00:03:08.200 --> 00:03:10.731
Then the special cases, on a new

00:03:10.731 --> 00:03:11.651
empty list.

00:03:11.651 --> 00:03:15.102
deleteFirst() on the empty list returns null

00:03:15.102 --> 00:03:16.712
instead of throwing.

00:03:16.712 --> 00:03:18.706
insertLast(42)

00:03:18.706 --> 00:03:21.160
where the slide version throws

00:03:21.160 --> 00:03:23.920
gives the one-node list, 42.

00:03:24.270 --> 00:03:27.199
deleteLast() on a list with a single

00:03:27.283 --> 00:03:29.458
node makes it empty again

00:03:29.458 --> 00:03:31.550
so isEmpty() prints true.

00:03:32.130 --> 00:03:33.660
The running times.

00:03:33.660 --> 00:03:34.850
insertFirst(),

00:03:34.850 --> 00:03:38.250
deleteFirst() and isEmpty() are O(1).

00:03:38.250 --> 00:03:42.075
insertLast() and deleteLast() walk to the end

00:03:42.075 --> 00:03:42.755
O(n).

00:03:42.755 --> 00:03:43.945
insertAfter(),

00:03:43.945 --> 00:03:47.430
deleteAfter() and search() walk to the key

00:03:47.430 --> 00:03:49.725
O(1) in the best case

00:03:49.725 --> 00:03:51.510
O(n) in the worst.

00:03:51.510 --> 00:03:54.570
Printing visits every node: O(n).

00:03:54.920 --> 00:03:57.159
Now two classic exercises

00:03:57.245 --> 00:03:59.829
both solved with more than one

00:03:59.915 --> 00:04:02.240
reference walking the list.

00:04:02.820 --> 00:04:03.814
Exercise 1:

00:04:03.814 --> 00:04:06.336
Given the head of a linked list,

00:04:06.336 --> 00:04:08.018
find its middle node.

00:04:08.018 --> 00:04:10.617
The obvious way takes two passes:

00:04:10.617 --> 00:04:13.751
count the nodes, then walk half of them.

00:04:13.751 --> 00:04:15.968
The neat way takes one pass

00:04:15.968 --> 00:04:17.420
with two pointers.

00:04:17.770 --> 00:04:21.050
Slow and fast both start at the head.

00:04:21.050 --> 00:04:22.291
In each step

00:04:22.291 --> 00:04:25.837
slow moves one node and fast moves two

00:04:25.837 --> 00:04:28.584
slow is at 2, fast at 3.

00:04:28.584 --> 00:04:32.130
Next step: slow at 3, fast at 5.

00:04:32.480 --> 00:04:34.586
Now fast.next is null

00:04:34.586 --> 00:04:36.126
so the loop stops.

00:04:36.126 --> 00:04:39.042
Fast has gone twice as far as slow

00:04:39.042 --> 00:04:41.716
so slow is in the middle: 3.

00:04:41.716 --> 00:04:45.280
People call this the tortoise and the hare.

00:04:45.860 --> 00:04:46.820
Now predict.

00:04:46.820 --> 00:04:50.020
With an even number of nodes, 1, 2,

00:04:50.020 --> 00:04:53.300
3, 4, there are two middle nodes.

00:04:53.300 --> 00:04:56.100
Which one does middleNode return?

00:04:56.100 --> 00:04:58.500
Pause the video and trace it.

00:05:02.080 --> 00:05:03.346
Start at 1.

00:05:03.346 --> 00:05:06.657
Step: slow at 2, fast at 3.

00:05:06.657 --> 00:05:09.870
Fast.next is 4, not null

00:05:09.870 --> 00:05:13.084
so one more step: slow at 3

00:05:13.084 --> 00:05:16.686
and fast jumps past the end to null.

00:05:16.686 --> 00:05:18.244
The loop stops

00:05:18.244 --> 00:05:21.263
and middleNode returns 3

00:05:21.263 --> 00:05:23.600
the second middle node.

00:05:23.950 --> 00:05:27.359
The slides' Java version, findMiddleNode

00:05:27.359 --> 00:05:30.848
returns the first middle node instead: 2.

00:05:30.927 --> 00:05:33.068
Both are correct answers to

00:05:33.147 --> 00:05:35.129
different specifications

00:05:35.129 --> 00:05:37.270
so say which one you want.

00:05:37.620 --> 00:05:40.100
Each step moves fast two nodes

00:05:40.100 --> 00:05:43.380
so the loop runs about n/2 times

00:05:43.380 --> 00:05:46.180
O(n) time, O(1) extra space

00:05:46.180 --> 00:05:47.220
in one pass.

00:05:47.220 --> 00:05:50.740
On an empty list, both methods return null.

00:05:51.320 --> 00:05:52.293
Exercise 2

00:05:52.293 --> 00:05:54.688
reverse a linked list in place

00:05:54.688 --> 00:05:57.383
by turning every next arrow around.

00:05:57.383 --> 00:05:59.553
Three references are needed.

00:05:59.553 --> 00:06:02.847
previousNode is the part already reversed

00:06:02.847 --> 00:06:04.269
it starts as null

00:06:04.269 --> 00:06:06.290
which will be the new end.

00:06:06.290 --> 00:06:09.433
currentNode is the node being processed

00:06:09.433 --> 00:06:11.080
starting at the head.

00:06:11.430 --> 00:06:14.065
First, save the rest of the list

00:06:14.065 --> 00:06:17.258
nextNode = currentNode.next.

00:06:17.258 --> 00:06:19.175
Then reverse this link

00:06:19.175 --> 00:06:22.688
currentNode.next = previousNode.

00:06:22.688 --> 00:06:25.642
Then move both pointers one node on

00:06:25.642 --> 00:06:28.516
previousNode becomes currentNode

00:06:28.516 --> 00:06:31.390
and currentNode becomes nextNode.

00:06:31.970 --> 00:06:34.560
The same four lines for 6:

00:06:34.560 --> 00:06:37.798
its arrow now points back to 5.

00:06:37.798 --> 00:06:40.850
For 7, for 1, and for 2.

00:06:41.200 --> 00:06:44.722
currentNode is null, so the loop ends

00:06:44.722 --> 00:06:48.605
and previousNode, the old last node, two

00:06:48.605 --> 00:06:50.141
is the new head.

00:06:50.141 --> 00:06:53.933
The old head, five, is now the last node

00:06:53.933 --> 00:06:55.920
and its next is null.

00:06:56.270 --> 00:06:59.164
Saving nextNode first is rule one

00:06:59.164 --> 00:07:01.975
it keeps the chain from breaking.

00:07:01.975 --> 00:07:04.208
Each node is visited once

00:07:04.208 --> 00:07:07.680
with three extra references: O(n) time

00:07:07.680 --> 00:07:09.830
and O(1) extra space.

00:07:10.410 --> 00:07:15.061
The demo prints 5, 6, 7, 1, 2

00:07:15.061 --> 00:07:19.711
and after reversing, 2, 1, 7, 6,

00:07:19.711 --> 00:07:20.360
5.

00:07:20.360 --> 00:07:25.010
And the old head, five, now links to null.

00:07:25.590 --> 00:07:26.456
To sum up.

00:07:26.456 --> 00:07:29.315
One: with only a head reference

00:07:29.402 --> 00:07:31.568
the front is O(1) and

00:07:31.654 --> 00:07:33.474
the end needs a walk

00:07:33.474 --> 00:07:35.900
check the empty list first.

00:07:35.900 --> 00:07:37.546
Two: two pointers

00:07:37.546 --> 00:07:40.318
one twice as fast as the other

00:07:40.318 --> 00:07:42.830
find the middle in one pass.

00:07:43.180 --> 00:07:44.563
Three: previous

00:07:44.563 --> 00:07:48.194
current and next reverse a list in place.

00:07:48.194 --> 00:07:51.220
Save next before you overwrite it.

00:07:51.570 --> 00:07:52.346
Try this.

00:07:52.346 --> 00:07:56.228
Return the k-th node from the end of a list

00:07:56.228 --> 00:07:57.349
in one pass

00:07:57.349 --> 00:08:00.713
where k equal to one is the last node.

00:08:00.713 --> 00:08:02.438
Hint: two pointers

00:08:02.438 --> 00:08:05.370
with the first one k nodes ahead.

00:08:05.720 --> 00:08:09.275
In part four: doubly linked, circular

00:08:09.275 --> 00:08:11.800
and doubly circular lists.

